(5472314, 785477, 97852, '<td valign="top">Beautiful Capture!  Way to go on the ribbon!  Love the colors, the posture and the bright face!</td>', 'Beautiful Capture!  Way to go on the ribbon!  Love the colors, the posture and the bright face!', '2009-05-06 10:34:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(5472333, 785430, 97852, '<td valign="top">Love the shot!  Had it ranked in my top group as one the ribbons.  Your focus is spot on and the 1/2000 shutter speed freezes it well.  Not sure why this one got so many 6''s.  Sometimes, I don''t think people appreciate or realize how hard it is to successfully capture birds in flight, especially raptors.  What is it?  A harrier?  Also, lens used?</td>', 'Love the shot!  Had it ranked in my top group as one the ribbons.  Your focus is spot on and the 1/2000 shutter speed freezes it well.  Not sure why this one got so many 6''s.  Sometimes, I don''t think people appreciate or realize how hard it is to successfully capture birds in flight, especially raptors.  What is it?  A harrier?  Also, lens used?', '2009-05-06 10:42:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(5472548, 784654, 49084, '<td valign="top">He look intense. This is an awsome shot. The eyes are beautiful. Did you use a tripod?</td>', 'He look intense. This is an awsome shot. The eyes are beautiful. Did you use a tripod?', '2009-05-06 12:26:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(5472999, 785453, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Well done for getting a good solid score in this challenge.  Always nice to score a six or higher. I am afraid I only rated this photo a 5, which may, on reflection be a bit tough (sometimes I wish we had a chance to go to more decimals).  <br/> <br/>Whilst I felt this captured the challenge topic pretty well, there were a few things that just stopped me giving it a 6.  I found the composition to be a bit too cramped.  I would like to have seen some more space around the dog, so it was more in context that it is.  <br/> <br/>The stick is hiding the dog''s eyes which is a pity.  Finally, for me, the lighting seemed to be a bit flat, making the colours a bit flat too.  I felt a tweak in the levels to brighten it up a bit and a slight boost to the saturation would have made this stand out a bit more.<br/> <br/>Anyway, you got a coveted Post Luminous award, so you know at least one person liked it a lot!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Well done for getting a good solid score in this challenge.  Always nice to score a six or higher. I am afraid I only rated this photo a 5, which may, on reflection be a bit tough (sometimes I wish we had a chance to go to more decimals).   Whilst I felt this captured the challenge topic pretty well, there were a few things that just stopped me giving it a 6.  I found the composition to be a bit too cramped.  I would like to have seen some more space around the dog, so it was more in context that it is.   The stick is hiding the dog''s eyes which is a pity.  Finally, for me, the lighting seemed to be a bit flat, making the colours a bit flat too.  I felt a tweak in the levels to brighten it up a bit and a slight boost to the saturation would have made this stand out a bit more. Anyway, you got a coveted Post Luminous award, so you know at least one person liked it a lot!', '2009-05-06 16:01:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(5473023, 784656, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>They look like they were really enjoying themselves.<br/> <br/>There are a few things that I think would have improved this image and garnered you a higher score.  Personally I think you have cropped the picture too much.  From a compositional point of view, I really think this would benefit with a bit more of the sea in the frame.  The picture feels quite small, again I think due to the cropping and resizing for the web.  Have you tried using the save for web functionality that comes with many editing packages?  This strips the exif data from the file this allowing you to retain better quality images when resizing for the web.  I noticed that the exif data was still present in your file, so possibly you would have had a better quality file, if you had saved it for the web.<br/> <br/>You got a pretty solid score for this image, I just felt with a few tweaks here and there, this could have scored a bit higher.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club They look like they were really enjoying themselves. There are a few things that I think would have improved this image and garnered you a higher score.  Personally I think you have cropped the picture too much.  From a compositional point of view, I really think this would benefit with a bit more of the sea in the frame.  The picture feels quite small, again I think due to the cropping and resizing for the web.  Have you tried using the save for web functionality that comes with many editing packages?  This strips the exif data from the file this allowing you to retain better quality images when resizing for the web.  I noticed that the exif data was still present in your file, so possibly you would have had a better quality file, if you had saved it for the web. You got a pretty solid score for this image, I just felt with a few tweaks here and there, this could have scored a bit higher.', '2009-05-06 16:11:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(5473062, 784825, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Sorry this did not score higher for you, I felt it was worthy of a higher score.  On the other hand, you have a <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=50695" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Posthumous</a> comment and an <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_N.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_N.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_N.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=3724" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Undieyatch</a> award, so hopefully those helped offset any disappointment from the score.<br/> <br/>I really liked the fact you actually showed some motion in your picture.  Too many in this challenge, for me at least, did not really do that, which was disappointing.  Converting to black and white was a good choice in my opinion.  I only wonder if perhaps it could have had a little bit less contrast as some of the details are lost due to the very dark shadows.  I do like the vignette, that worked very well.<br/> <br/>Overall, I thought this was well composed, a good subject that definitely met the challenge.  The only reason I didn''t score it higher, was I felt it could have had a little bit less contrast.<br/> <br/>Well done and again, sorry it didn''t score higher.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Sorry this did not score higher for you, I felt it was worthy of a higher score.  On the other hand, you have a  Posthumous comment and an  Undieyatch award, so hopefully those helped offset any disappointment from the score. I really liked the fact you actually showed some motion in your picture.  Too many in this challenge, for me at least, did not really do that, which was disappointing.  Converting to black and white was a good choice in my opinion.  I only wonder if perhaps it could have had a little bit less contrast as some of the details are lost due to the very dark shadows.  I do like the vignette, that worked very well. Overall, I thought this was well composed, a good subject that definitely met the challenge.  The only reason I didn''t score it higher, was I felt it could have had a little bit less contrast. Well done and again, sorry it didn''t score higher.', '2009-05-06 16:21:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(5473468, 785584, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Initial impression: Basically a good shot, just too much building and too little bird.<br/> <br/>Technical: Basially good concept and shot, used ambient lighting, would have scored much higher if bird''s feet were in view, maybe some building undeneath bird feet.  Glare on wings may have hurt too.  <br/> <br/>Artistic: I like this shot in terms of POV, dof good but probably a little soft. Looks too much like a snapshot. Next time, just take a deep breath, relax, and hold down the shutter release button.  Catch the bird climbing upwards. If you have time, set camera to continuous mode (check manual) and try that.<br/> <br/>Overall: a very good attempt at a fairly tough challenge, cause voters want to see a clear shot of the subject but an oof background. You gave them both, but it''s simply too low in the shot to make an impact.<br/> <br/>FWIW: Keep shooting.  Don''t just shoot one shot of anything, shoot at least a dozen.  Then look at them carefully, individually, and find the one that keeps cropping up and resonates with you as the best shot, the best capture.  Then enter it.<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me with any questions,<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Initial impression: Basically a good shot, just too much building and too little bird. Technical: Basially good concept and shot, used ambient lighting, would have scored much higher if bird''s feet were in view, maybe some building undeneath bird feet.  Glare on wings may have hurt too.   Artistic: I like this shot in terms of POV, dof good but probably a little soft. Looks too much like a snapshot. Next time, just take a deep breath, relax, and hold down the shutter release button.  Catch the bird climbing upwards. If you have time, set camera to continuous mode (check manual) and try that. Overall: a very good attempt at a fairly tough challenge, cause voters want to see a clear shot of the subject but an oof background. You gave them both, but it''s simply too low in the shot to make an impact. FWIW: Keep shooting.  Don''t just shoot one shot of anything, shoot at least a dozen.  Then look at them carefully, individually, and find the one that keeps cropping up and resonates with you as the best shot, the best capture.  Then enter it. Feel free to PM me with any questions, Susan', '2009-05-06 19:55:54.000000', NULL, 0),
